.th NARC I 12/17/75
.sh NAME
narc \*- source archiver
.sh SYNOPSIS
.bd narc
file -k1 a11 ... a1n ... -km am1 ... amn
.sh DESCRIPTION
	narc____ is a revision of the unix archiver (ar__ (i)) to exploit the
.it Narc
is a revision of
.it ar
(I) to exploit
the
new file system.
The action of
.it narc
is determined by a left-to-right
scan of the command.
The valid keys are:
.s3
.ft I
.lp +17 16
key	meaning
.ft R
.s3
.lp +17 16
-d f1 ... fn	The named files are deleted from the library.
.s3
.lp +17 16
-r f1 ... fn	The named files are replaced.
.s3
.lp +17 16
-m f1 ... fn	The named files are replaced if their modification date is more
recent than the copy in the archive.
.s3
.lp +17 16
-i k f1 ... fn	The named files are placed before the \fIk\fR file in the library.
.s3
.lp +17 16
-x f1 ... fn	The named file are extracted from the library.
If no files are specified, then
all files are extracted.
.s3
.lp +17 16
-a f1 ... fn	The named files are appended to the archive.
If the archive does not exist, it is
created.
.s3
.lp +17 16
-l	The archive contents are listed.
.i0
.dt
.sh FILES
narc.tmp		work file
.sh BUGS
Generally, file names must be in the correct order.
This is rediculous, and
.it narc
should be fixed to eliminate this.
